1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,110
This is a simple story about one company, NVIDIA,

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,280
and one investment idea.

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:10,630
Today, millions of people use AI.

4
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,850
You ask a question, and a computer answers.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:17,740
Even an 80-year-old retiree can use it for help.

6
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:24,700
NVIDIA was started in 1993 by Jensen Huang and two partners.

7
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:27,630
At first, they made chips for video games.

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,530
Those chips are called GPUs.

9
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,000
A regular chip handles a few jobs at a time.

10
00:00:35,650 --> 00:00:39,380
A GPU handles thousands of small jobs at once.

11
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:42,170
And that is exactly what AI needs.

12
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,460
Today, NVIDIA is a leading supplier of these chips

13
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:52,290
for giant computer buildings called data centers, where AI runs.

14
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:55,600
Let's connect the dots:

15
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:59,820
video games, to GPUs, to AI, to data centers.

16
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:03,210
And the business has grown.

17
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:08,630
In three recent quarters, sales went from $68.1 billion,

18
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:13,150
to $81.6 billion, to $96.2 billion.

19
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:17,590
Many large companies work with NVIDIA.

20
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:20,430
Soon, it will report its next results.

21
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,110
News like that can move the share price — up or down.

22
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:30,370
Now, an example. Say one share costs $218.

23
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:36,900
If the price rises 8%, it becomes $235.44.

24
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:42,540
This example uses borrowed money. That is called leverage.

25
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:45,790
You put in $25,000 of your own,

26
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:49,890
the broker lends you about $100,000 more,

27
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:53,710
and together you buy 572 shares.

28
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:58,520
By investing $25,000 this way,

29
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:06,010
you could potentially make $9,975.68, before costs.

30
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,330
With $50,000 of your own and more borrowing, you buy 1,720 shares.

31
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:20,820
The same rise could make $29,996.80.

32
00:02:22,830 --> 00:02:24,450
But borrowing works both ways.

33
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:28,790
If the price falls 8% instead, you lose the same amounts.

34
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:35,350
The loan costs interest and fees, and if prices fall far enough, the broker can sell your shares.

35
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:41,170
These are examples, not promises, and not financial advice.

36
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:44,310
Before investing, talk with someone you trust.
